WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
Cross-field rules and abstention.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:04.035
Evidence checks look at one field.

3
00:00:04.315 --> 00:00:09.221
Many errors only show up when fields are compared with each other or with facts you hold.

4
00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:18.867
Write these as plain functions, run after validation, with one named rule each: The owner must be someone in the meeting (or in your team directory).

5
00:00:19.147 --> 00:00:23.883
An unknown name is either a hallucination or a person you need to resolve.

6
00:00:24.163 --> 00:00:26.403
A due date can’t be before the meeting date.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:35.792
If the transcript says “Friday,” resolve it in code from the meeting date, as the project requires, rather than trusting the model’s calendar arithmetic.

8
00:00:36.072 --> 00:00:39.741
If an item says “done,” it shouldn’t also have a future due date.

9
00:00:40.021 --> 00:00:43.520
The owner should be the speaker of the quoted line, or be named in it.

10
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.448
This one is a heuristic; use it to flag, not to reject.

11
00:00:47.728 --> 00:00:55.771
Each rule is cheap, deterministic, and testable, and each catches a class of error that no amount of prompting reliably prevents.

12
00:00:56.051 --> 00:00:59.848
The other half is giving the model a legal way to say “I don’t know.

13
00:01:00.128 --> 00:01:06.379
” Anthropic’s first recommendation for reducing hallucinations is to explicitly allow uncertainty.

14
00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:16.792
In a schema, that means abstention fields: make owner and due_date nullable, and add a short reason when they’re null (“no one volunteered”, “date discussed but not agreed”).

15
00:01:17.072 --> 00:01:18.971
Abstentions are useful data.

16
00:01:19.251 --> 00:01:27.037
A sudden jump in null owners tells you the transcripts changed or the prompt broke; a reason string tells a reviewer what to check.
